// Karimu Water Assets registry — embedded so the Ward/Village/Sub-Village/
// Asset Tag picker (and the volunteer route/map screen) work fully offline,
// matching the rest of this app's offline-first design.
//
// Source: Karimu "Water Assets" Google Sheet, "Water Assets" tab
// (https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1HLnqnP8wUJjV-MfVO9o7cio693qG220MpOHbFLRLrFE),
// exported 2026-08-25. 1,717 assets across the Ayalagaya, Arri, and Kiru
// wards — 1,693 Water Points and 24 Water Tanks. Only Water Points are
// surfaced in the picker right now (see the filter on WATER_ASSETS below).
// Re-export and regenerate this file whenever the sheet changes
// significantly (new water points, renamed villages, new GPS coordinates,
// ownership changes).
//
// Each row is a tuple to keep this file's size down:
//   [assetTag, assetType, ward, village, subVillage, location,
//    lat, lon, volunteer, routeOrder, isPublic, group]
// assetType is "P" for Water Point or "T" for Water Tank — this decides
// which checklist (WATER_POINT_SECTIONS or WATER_TANK_SECTIONS) applies.
// ward/village/subVillage/location are the raw values from the sheet;
// two Kiru assets have no village/sub-village on file and use "".
//
// isPublic is 1 when the sheet's "Ownership" column says "Public", 0 for
// "Private" — as of the 2026-08-25 export, 332 of the 1,693 Water Points
// are Public and 1,361 are Private (all 24 Water Tanks are Public). Only
// Public Water Points are surfaced anywhere in this app (picker, route,
// map) — Edu's scope decision, confirmed 2026-08-25: private water points
// aren't part of Karimu's audit program. The raw rows for private points
// stay in this file either way (same reversibility pattern as the Water
// Tank filter above); dropping the isPublic check in WATER_ASSETS brings
// them back if that scope ever changes.
//
// lat/lon come from the sheet's "Coordinates in decimal" column and are
// null when that column was blank there.
//
// --- Groups and routing (regenerated 2026-09-13, second pass) ---
// volunteer/routeOrder/group are precomputed here (not at runtime) so
// every device shows the exact same assignment. They come straight from
// Edu's "Water Points" routing spreadsheet (the "Edu 50th birthday
// (Karimu)" sheet, tab "Water Points"), re-exported after the routes were
// redone: 312 Public Water Points in 24 hand-planned groups (10-14 points
// each), worked over 4 field days, 6 groups per day
// (group -> day = Math.ceil(group / 6), i.e. groups 1-6 on day 1, 7-12 on
// day 2, 13-18 on day 3, 19-24 on day 4), with the same 6 volunteers
// covering one group per day each: Thuler -> groups 1/7/13/19, Padilha ->
// 2/8/14/20, Gui Lacerda -> 3/9/15/21, Rafa Braga -> 4/10/16/22, Arake ->
// 5/11/17/23, Sergio -> 6/12/18/24. routeOrder is the visiting order
// *within that volunteer's group* (the sheet's row order within the
// group), used to draw "My route"'s suggested path.
// Each regeneration fully replaces the previous plan: every Water Point
// assigned under the old plan is reset to null first, so points dropped
// from the new sheet don't keep a stale group.
//
// HOW TO RE-EXPORT THIS (the naive way loses data — read this first):
// the Drive connector's read_file_content on a workbook this large
// silently TRUNCATES the "Water Points" tab partway through (it cut off
// inside group 12 on 2026-09-13, which produced a bogus 12-group/149-point
// plan that had to be redone). Pull the tab on its own instead, e.g. the
// gviz CSV endpoint for that one sheet:
//   /spreadsheets/d/<id>/gviz/tq?tqx=out:csv&headers=1&sheet=Water%20Points
// Note that endpoint drops the sheet's "Group N" label rows, so segment
// the groups by where the row index restarts at 1 / the volunteer name
// changes, then number those segments 1..N in order — that reproduces the
// sheet's own group numbering. Segmenting on the index reset ALONE is not
// enough: at least one group (17) starts on a row whose index cell is
// blank, so the volunteer-name change is what catches that boundary.
// Always sanity-check the result before committing: 6 volunteers x 4
// groups each, group sizes in the 10-14 range, every assigned tag
// resolving to a Public Water Point in this registry, and NO duplicate
// tags. A duplicate means the sheet lists the same point twice in a group
// — that's a data-entry error to send back to Karimu, not something to
// paper over here: on 2026-09-13 group 21 had DABWP3 twice, which made
// Gui's group show 13 points instead of 14 until Nelson replaced the
// second copy with the point that was actually meant to be there.

// "Teste" (added 2026-08-26) is a QA/testing identity, not a real
// volunteer — it has no Water Points assigned in WATER_ASSET_ROWS below,
// so its "My route" is intentionally empty (the app already handles a
// volunteer with zero assigned points: see the empty-state text in
// screenRoute() in lib/engine.js).
const VOLUNTEERS = ["Thuler", "Arake", "Gui Lacerda", "Rafa Braga", "Sergio", "Padilha", "Teste"];

/** Which field day a group is worked on: groups 1-6 -> day 1, 7-12 -> day
 * 2, 13-18 -> day 3, 19-24 -> day 4. */
function groupDay(group){
  return Math.ceil(group / 6);
}

